1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,112
The first promise of the arc was made in the innovation lab, with a keyboard, a mouse, and a printer on the table.

2
00:00:07,112 --> 00:00:13,037
“Every system we access will have an argument behind it,” Maya said.

3
00:00:13,037 --> 00:00:26,772
“A program is not a promise; a promise is a program with a reason.” We would take the third position: not the base of the network, but the code at the door.

4
00:00:26,772 --> 00:00:35,006
The physics of the promise was simple: energy in, data out, and no chemical left behind.

5
00:00:35,006 --> 00:00:48,606
A cell of six volunteers, a machine that nobody had switched off, and an operation manual in a format nobody could read: the effort of the first week was to leave nothing open.

6
00:00:48,606 --> 00:00:53,401
“You cannot calculate trust,” the engineer said.

7
00:00:53,401 --> 00:00:56,816
“You can only monitor it.”

8
00:00:56,816 --> 00:01:00,366
The event that made it urgent was a virus.

9
00:01:00,366 --> 00:01:13,575
A bug in the driver, a worm in the backup, and a crash of the printer on the night before the launch: the track of the damage led to a dump of old files that had never been registered.

10
00:01:13,575 --> 00:01:18,125
“A scanner is not a conscience,” Aisha wrote.

11
00:01:18,125 --> 00:01:22,160
“An indicator is not a control.”

12
00:01:22,160 --> 00:01:27,325
The position was difficult, and it had to be provided in writing.

13
00:01:27,325 --> 00:01:45,774
Every device, every software package, and every online service would have a named person responsible for it; the hardware in the lab would be inventoried, and the logic of every access rule would be explained to the volunteers in one page.

14
00:01:45,774 --> 00:01:53,118
The technology was not the point, and even the biotechnology group in the next building knew it.

15
00:01:53,118 --> 00:02:05,427
A hacker in cyberspace, a register of permissions, and a network that remembered more than any human could: thus the phase of the design had to begin with deletion.

16
00:02:05,427 --> 00:02:16,672
“Create less,” Lin wrote, “and the Internet will have less to remember.” It was a promise about forgetting, and it arrived on time.

17
00:02:16,672 --> 00:02:27,222
We have a difficult effort ahead: to create a base, to take the third pass at the code, and to reach a position we can provide in writing.

18
00:02:27,222 --> 00:02:33,417
Down the corridor the night shift was playing music, or at least something like it.

19
00:02:33,417 --> 00:02:39,346
Thus the event of the launch had to leave a year of work in a form that could survive us.

20
00:02:39,346 --> 00:02:50,880
A computer, a source, a science, and an engineering team: the club had none of them, and the code was written by volunteers.
